<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eudoxus of Cnidus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 408 – c. 355 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eudoxus of Cnidus was a Greek mathematician and astronomer of the 4th century BCE. He developed a theory of proportion and the method of exhaustion, later used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>'s Elements, and devised the first geometrical model of the motions of the planets, a system of nested spheres centred on the Earth. He also described the constellations and taught at his own school at Cyzicus.</p></div>
